1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">Recurrent neural networks are a workhorse for
sequence data,</font>

2
00:00:04,420 --> 00:00:09,045
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet they famously struggle when the data carries
long-term dependencies.</font>

3
00:00:09,125 --> 00:00:13,814
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper asks why, from the rigorous viewpoint of
approximation theory.</font>

4
00:00:13,894 --> 00:00:16,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors prove an inverse, or Bernstein-type,</font>

5
00:00:17,031 --> 00:00:22,504
<font color="#FFFFFF">theorem for nonlinear RNNs: any sequence
relationship that can be stably approximated</font>

6
00:00:22,584 --> 00:00:26,621
<font color="#FFFFFF">by an RNN must have a memory that decays
exponentially in time.</font>

7
00:00:26,701 --> 00:00:31,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">In other words, the difficulty is not just an
optimization artifact,</font>

8
00:00:31,144 --> 00:00:34,919
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is a fundamental limitation of the RNN
hypothesis space,</font>

9
00:00:34,999 --> 00:00:39,492
<font color="#FFFFFF">extending the known curse of memory from linear to
nonlinear networks.</font>

10
00:00:39,572 --> 00:00:41,191
<font color="#FFFFFF">Building on this analysis,</font>

11
00:00:41,271 --> 00:00:47,006
<font color="#FFFFFF">they propose a principled stable reparameterization
that lets RNNs escape the limitation,</font>

12
00:00:47,086 --> 00:00:50,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they confirm everything with numerical experiments.</font>

13
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:57,424
<font color="#FFFFFF">Recurrent neural networks are among the most basic
models for learning from sequential and temporal data,</font>

14
00:00:57,504 --> 00:01:01,649
<font color="#FFFFFF">with applications from time series and speech to
text and sentiment.</font>

15
00:01:01,729 --> 00:01:08,049
<font color="#FFFFFF">But a long-standing empirical observation is that
they falter when the data has long-term dependencies.</font>

16
00:01:08,129 --> 00:01:13,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">The open question this paper confronts is whether
that failure is only about training dynamics,</font>

17
00:01:14,031 --> 00:01:18,363
<font color="#FFFFFF">like exploding or vanishing gradients, or whether
it reflects a deeper,</font>

18
00:01:18,443 --> 00:01:21,842
<font color="#FFFFFF">structural limitation of what RNNs can represent at all.</font>

19
00:01:21,922 --> 00:01:27,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">Answering this requires an approximation-theoretic
lens rather than an optimization one.</font>

20
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:31,936
<font color="#FFFFFF">Approximation theory offers two complementary kinds
of statements.</font>

21
00:01:32,016 --> 00:01:33,574
<font color="#FFFFFF">Forward, or Jackson-type,</font>

22
00:01:33,654 --> 00:01:38,884
<font color="#FFFFFF">theorems tell you how well a model can approximate
a sufficiently regular target.</font>

23
00:01:38,964 --> 00:01:40,653
<font color="#FFFFFF">Inverse, or Bernstein-type,</font>

24
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:46,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">theorems run the other direction: they assume a
target can be efficiently approximated</font>

25
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:49,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">and then deduce what regularity the target must have.</font>

26
00:01:49,844 --> 00:01:56,056
<font color="#FFFFFF">Inverse theorems are precisely the tool for
exposing fundamental limitations of an architecture.</font>

27
00:01:56,136 --> 00:01:59,333
<font color="#FFFFFF">Earlier work proved such a result for linear RNNs,</font>

28
00:01:59,413 --> 00:02:05,429
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that efficiently approximable linear
targets must have exponentially decaying memory.</font>

29
00:02:05,509 --> 00:02:08,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">The pressing question was whether adding nonlinearity,</font>

30
00:02:09,048 --> 00:02:14,408
<font color="#FFFFFF">which greatly increases model capacity, would break
this so-called curse of memory.</font>

31
00:02:14,488 --> 00:02:17,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper was motivated to settle that.</font>

32
00:02:17,410 --> 00:02:20,934
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

33
00:02:21,014 --> 00:02:24,537
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

34
00:02:24,617 --> 00:02:28,141
<font color="#FFFFFF">it extends the concept of a memory function from
the linear setting to general nonlinear functional sequences,</font>

35
00:02:28,221 --> 00:02:31,744
<font color="#FFFFFF">and crucially this memory function can be
numerically quantified by querying a trained model,</font>

36
00:02:31,824 --> 00:02:35,348
<font color="#FFFFFF">not just defined abstractly.</font>

37
00:02:35,428 --> 00:02:38,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it introduces a framework of stable approximation,</font>

38
00:02:39,031 --> 00:02:42,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">a mild requirement that the approximant behaves
continuously under small parameter perturbations,</font>

39
00:02:42,635 --> 00:02:46,159
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is exactly what gradient-based optimization needs.</font>

40
00:02:46,239 --> 00:02:49,762
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

41
00:02:49,842 --> 00:02:53,366
<font color="#FFFFFF">using these two ingredients it proves what the
authors believe is the first Bernstein-type</font>

42
00:02:53,446 --> 00:02:56,969
<font color="#FFFFFF">approximation theorem for nonlinear RNNs.</font>

43
00:02:57,049 --> 00:03:00,573
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of the theory,</font>

44
00:03:00,653 --> 00:03:04,176
<font color="#FFFFFF">it proposes a principled reparameterization method
to overcome the identified limitation</font>

45
00:03:04,256 --> 00:03:07,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">and confirms the whole story with experiments.</font>

46
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:12,208
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method has three pieces.</font>

47
00:03:12,288 --> 00:03:16,336
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, a memory function for nonlinear functionals:
it measures,</font>

48
00:03:16,416 --> 00:03:20,463
<font color="#FFFFFF">over Heaviside step inputs,</font>

49
00:03:20,543 --> 00:03:24,591
<font color="#FFFFFF">how strongly the output at time t still depends on
the input, giving a precise,</font>

50
00:03:24,671 --> 00:03:28,719
<font color="#FFFFFF">task-independent, and numerically queryable notion
of memory.</font>

51
00:03:28,799 --> 00:03:32,847
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

52
00:03:32,927 --> 00:03:36,974
<font color="#FFFFFF">a stability framework: the perturbation error at
hidden dimension m is the worst-case</font>

53
00:03:37,054 --> 00:03:41,102
<font color="#FFFFFF">output error when parameters are perturbed within a
ball of radius beta,</font>

54
00:03:41,182 --> 00:03:45,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a target is beta-zero stably approximated if
that error stays continuous up to some positive radius.</font>

55
00:03:45,310 --> 00:03:49,358
<font color="#FFFFFF">The central theorem then says: if a target with
decaying memory is stably approximated</font>

56
00:03:49,438 --> 00:03:53,486
<font color="#FFFFFF">by RNNs whose weight norms and perturbed memories
stay uniformly controlled,</font>

57
00:03:53,566 --> 00:03:57,613
<font color="#FFFFFF">then the target's memory must decay exponentially.</font>

58
00:03:57,693 --> 00:04:01,741
<font color="#FFFFFF">The proof leverages stability to drive hidden-state
derivatives to zero and uses the Hartman-Grobman</font>

59
00:04:01,821 --> 00:04:05,869
<font color="#FFFFFF">theorem to bound the recurrent eigenvalues.</font>

60
00:04:05,949 --> 00:04:09,997
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally, to overcome this curse,</font>

61
00:04:10,077 --> 00:04:14,124
<font color="#FFFFFF">they substitute the recurrent weight matrix with a
continuous function that always maps into stable,</font>

62
00:04:14,204 --> 00:04:18,252
<font color="#FFFFFF">negative-real-part matrices, such as an exponential
or softplus map,</font>

63
00:04:18,332 --> 00:04:22,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the network can push eigenvalues toward zero
while remaining stable under perturbation.</font>

64
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:26,416
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments span synthetic and real data.</font>

65
00:04:26,496 --> 00:04:30,152
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the synthetic side,</font>

66
00:04:30,232 --> 00:04:33,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors build linear and nonlinear functional
targets with either exponential or polynomial</font>

67
00:04:33,968 --> 00:04:37,623
<font color="#FFFFFF">decaying memory and sweep the hidden dimension from
about two up to sixty four to watch</font>

68
00:04:37,703 --> 00:04:41,359
<font color="#FFFFFF">how the perturbation-stability radius behaves.</font>

69
00:04:41,439 --> 00:04:45,095
<font color="#FFFFFF">They also construct randomly initialized RNN
teacher models with a large hidden dimension</font>

70
00:04:45,175 --> 00:04:48,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">of two hundred fifty six and approximate them with
student RNNs to test the stability</font>

71
00:04:48,911 --> 00:04:52,567
<font color="#FFFFFF">filter.</font>

72
00:04:52,647 --> 00:04:56,303
<font color="#FFFFFF">On real data,</font>

73
00:04:56,383 --> 00:05:00,038
<font color="#FFFFFF">they query the memory function of LSTM models on
IMDB movie-review sentiment analysis,</font>

74
00:05:00,118 --> 00:05:03,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they train nonlinear RNNs on MNIST image
classification to test the optimization benefits</font>

75
00:05:03,854 --> 00:05:07,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">of stable reparameterization.</font>

76
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:13,572
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline theoretical result is a clean inverse
statement: if a nonlinear RNN can stably</font>

77
00:05:13,652 --> 00:05:16,295
<font color="#FFFFFF">approximate a target sequence relationship,</font>

78
00:05:16,375 --> 00:05:19,524
<font color="#FFFFFF">then that target's memory must decay exponentially.</font>

79
00:05:19,604 --> 00:05:24,969
<font color="#FFFFFF">This extends the linear curse of memory to the
nonlinear regime and proves the failure</font>

80
00:05:25,049 --> 00:05:29,212
<font color="#FFFFFF">on long-term dependencies is intrinsic to the RNN
hypothesis space,</font>

81
00:05:29,292 --> 00:05:31,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">not just a training artifact.</font>

82
00:05:31,128 --> 00:05:32,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">The numerics back this up.</font>

83
00:05:32,774 --> 00:05:34,594
<font color="#FFFFFF">For polynomial-memory targets,</font>

84
00:05:34,674 --> 00:05:40,103
<font color="#FFFFFF">the perturbation-error curves for different hidden
dimensions keep intersecting further</font>

85
00:05:40,183 --> 00:05:42,635
<font color="#FFFFFF">and further left as the dimension grows,</font>

86
00:05:42,715 --> 00:05:45,548
<font color="#FFFFFF">meaning no positive stability radius survives.</font>

87
00:05:45,628 --> 00:05:50,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">And when the authors filter randomly generated
teacher models to keep only those that</font>

88
00:05:51,010 --> 00:05:53,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">are both approximable and stable,</font>

89
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:57,199
<font color="#FFFFFF">the only survivors are teachers whose memory decays
exponentially.</font>

90
00:05:57,279 --> 00:06:02,581
<font color="#FFFFFF">They also observe that slowly-decaying targets take
on the order of a thousand epochs</font>

91
00:06:02,661 --> 00:06:05,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">to fit versus about ten for exponential ones.</font>

92
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:10,419
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key ablation varies only how the recurrent
weight is parameterized,</font>

93
00:06:10,499 --> 00:06:12,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">holding initialization fixed.</font>

94
00:06:12,414 --> 00:06:17,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">On MNIST, the three stable reparameterizations,
softplus, exponential, and inverse,</font>

95
00:06:17,895 --> 00:06:22,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">all speed up optimization and reach higher test
accuracy than the direct,</font>

96
00:06:22,716 --> 00:06:26,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">unstable parameterization where the weight is used as-is.</font>

97
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:31,948
<font color="#FFFFFF">Importantly, since reparameterization does not
change the model's inherent capacity,</font>

98
00:06:32,028 --> 00:06:35,844
<font color="#FFFFFF">the final performance across stable variants is
comparable,</font>

99
00:06:35,924 --> 00:06:41,919
<font color="#FFFFFF">isolating the effect as an optimization and
stability benefit rather than a capacity change.</font>

100
00:06:41,999 --> 00:06:43,372
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the synthetic side,</font>

101
00:06:43,452 --> 00:06:49,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">applying exponential and softplus
reparameterizations to linear RNNs approximating polynomial-decay</font>

102
00:06:49,990 --> 00:06:54,005
<font color="#FFFFFF">targets restores the expected continuous limiting
error curve,</font>

103
00:06:54,085 --> 00:06:56,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming stability is recovered.</font>

104
00:06:56,630 --> 00:06:58,693
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

105
00:06:58,773 --> 00:07:02,719
<font color="#FFFFFF">On MNIST, trained for ten epochs and averaged over
three runs,</font>

106
00:07:02,799 --> 00:07:09,018
<font color="#FFFFFF">the softplus stable reparameterization reaches
seventy one point three six percent test accuracy,</font>

107
00:07:09,098 --> 00:07:14,537
<font color="#FFFFFF">compared with sixty eight point four seven percent
for the direct, unstable baseline,</font>

108
00:07:14,617 --> 00:07:17,719
<font color="#FFFFFF">an improvement of nearly three percentage points.</font>

109
00:07:17,799 --> 00:07:22,394
<font color="#FFFFFF">The exponential and inverse stable maps also land
above seventy percent,</font>

110
00:07:22,474 --> 00:07:24,537
<font color="#FFFFFF">all beating the unstable version.</font>

111
00:07:24,617 --> 00:07:29,732
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the synthetic side, exponential-memory targets
are fit in roughly ten epochs,</font>

112
00:07:29,812 --> 00:07:36,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">while polynomial-memory targets need on the order
of a thousand epochs and still fail the stability test.</font>

113
00:07:36,630 --> 00:07:41,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">The teacher-filtering experiment uses hidden
dimension two hundred fifty six.</font>

114
00:07:41,930 --> 00:07:45,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">The one-line takeaway is this: no matter how you
train them,</font>

115
00:07:45,746 --> 00:07:52,344
<font color="#FFFFFF">plain nonlinear RNNs can only stably approximate
sequence relationships whose memory fades exponentially,</font>

116
00:07:52,424 --> 00:07:57,941
<font color="#FFFFFF">so their well-known struggle with long-term
dependencies is baked into the architecture,</font>

117
00:07:58,021 --> 00:07:59,532
<font color="#FFFFFF">not merely the optimizer.</font>

118
00:07:59,612 --> 00:08:02,139
<font color="#FFFFFF">But the same analysis points to the cure.</font>

119
00:08:02,219 --> 00:08:07,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">By reparameterizing the recurrent weights with a
stable map like exponential or softplus,</font>

120
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:12,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">the network can keep eigenvalues near the edge of
stability without losing it,</font>

121
00:08:12,841 --> 00:08:17,086
<font color="#FFFFFF">provably relaxing the curse of memory and, on real
tasks like MNIST,</font>

122
00:08:17,166 --> 00:08:19,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">training faster and generalizing better.</font>
